Purpose
--------
This directory comprises a 3 router topology. Some links have asymmetric
weights i.e., directed edges with different costs. Each router is also
attached to its LAN comprising one PC each.

For this scaffolding code, we enable OSPF (which uses the link state
routing algorithm based on Dijkstra's shortest path)

The lab itself is described in the frrlab.clab.yml file. It uses
all linux kind nodes. The routers run the FRR image while the
user end-devices run a small Alpine Linux-based image that has
numerous networking utilities pre-installed.

Directory structure
--------------------
Each router directory has two files:
  daemons: this enables the routing daemons we need for this
           scaffolding (zebra and ospf)
  frr.conf: has substantial number of details on the configuration
            of that router including all its interfaces and link
            weights. Please study carefully.
  
To make the shell scripts provided in this directory executable,
run "chmod u+x *.sh"

Running the lab and Experimenting
---------------------------------
To run the lab, execute "./run.sh"

You can view routing tables at each PC or router as follows:

docker exec -it <container name> route -n
  where container name is something like  clab-frrlab-router1

You can check if your packet will take the shortest path or not
using the following command:

docker exec -it clab-frrlab-pc1 traceroute 172.16.2.2
(here, although there is a direct connection from router 1 to
router 2, the route taken will take us from PC1 to router 1 to
router 3 to router 2 to PC2 because that is shortest path)

Terminating
-----------
To terminate the lab, execute "./cleanup.sh"

Additional Experimentation
--------------------------
feel free to change the weights; add new routers; remove
any link etc and get a feel for the way everything works.
